> I was wondering whether there is a potential market for a shareware
> graphical roguelike?

Commercial graphical games inspired by Nogue and Nethack
include dozens or hundreds of popular games from Castle
Wolfenstein on.  The market is huge, with specific caveats.

> Or do all the roguelike fans prefer free and opensource stuff.

That's one of the cavaets.  You either have to rebuild the
program from scratch to make it commercial or add what
you like and make it free.  How shareware fits into that
spectrum is not a topic I know about.

Fans of roguelikes do prefer free and also don't prefer
graphical.  The usual idea is effort put into graphics is
effort subtracted from game play.

The graphical games often had huge markets, but not a
one of them has lasted for decades.  They don't compete
in even remotely the same space.  If you want to tap into
some middle space between those two highly successfull
games, go for it.



